Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
 "Open AI ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["-", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "64b83331-8650-4a21-a030-ee724209cfc5"],
 " Generative language modeling for automated theorem proving"
}], "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.965203992582182*^9, 3.96520400157378*^9}, {
  3.965204838243374*^9, 
  3.965204840638007*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"f9392e54-fcc1-46ad-b7f8-\
3419ee5f9728"],

Cell["\<\
This work is motivated by the possibility that a major limitation
of automated theorem provers compared to humans \[Dash] the generation of \
original mathematical terms \[Dash] might be addressable via generation from \
language models. We present an automated prover and proof assistant, GPT-f, \
for the Metamath formalization language, and analyze its performance. GPT-f \
found new short proofs that were accepted into the main Metamath library, \
which is to our knowledge, the first time a deep learning based system has \
contributed proofs that were adopted by a formal mathematics community. \
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9652040458826838`*^9, 
  3.965204055135388*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"dee8eed8-fd0d-415d-9e29-\
f68601774af3"],

Cell["\<\
How one might build an automated theorem prover? Well, it\[CloseCurlyQuote]s \
kind of just like a rewrite system. But a lot of those theorems might just be \
very obvious. Thus, there is some sort of motivation for some future proofs. \
For instance, there are some questions we ask which narrow our search. Then \
we do things. Maybe language has the ability to encode such intelligence. \
Sometimes proofs are found by running various examples and stuff. It is \
interesting to see how far deductive logic will take someone. \
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.965204056369335*^9, 3.965204072536632*^9}, {
  3.9652041117356663`*^9, 3.9652041902752*^9}, {3.965204362701877*^9, 
  3.96520438206418*^9}, {3.965204747157045*^9, 
  3.965204747744142*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"e28e7d10-a8e3-4b74-b6aa-\
04d56838b5c0"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
 "Harrison ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["-", TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "7a66fedc-b464-45e8-a4a4-1a53ca8013b9"],
 " History of interactive theorem proving"
}], "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.965204807412856*^9, 3.965204819034409*^9}, {
  3.965221619137774*^9, 
  3.965221620110414*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"ce8a988f-92c1-4086-b229-\
4e86b51a7207"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "In order to guide a machine proof, there needs to be a language for the \
user to communicate that proof to the machine, and designing an effective and \
convenient language is non",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["-", TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "12c9920e-4d94-430b-888c-602563d5fff2"],
 "trivial.  Moreover, early computers were typically ",
 StyleBox["batch-oriented",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ", often with very limited facilities for interaction."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.96520504289997*^9, 3.965205062368178*^9}, {
  3.9652051886749487`*^9, 
  3.965205190638255*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a6ce6aee-4ace-4e57-921b-\
c7ffab17f62a"]
}, Closed]],

