 CellLabel->"Out[18]=",ExpressionUUID->"8b1652b2-3e21-460c-bf07-4e3a3708a226"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["I\[CloseCurlyQuote]ve completed half the protein", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.968575195324243*^9, 
  3.968575198820094*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"8f2cad7c-1337-4447-b112-\
79857642ebfd"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["Model based reinforcement learning for ATARI", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.968642389057499*^9, 
  3.9686423963188*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"2017d9cc-d1c2-4c37-ac98-1c34b53acad1"],

Cell["\<\
Data for Mathematical Copilots: Better Ways of Presenting Proofs for Machine \
Learning\
\>", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9690930433794727`*^9, 
  3.969093044409347*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"ef6056c3-1720-4324-be87-\
4859158b399c"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Podcast with Yang", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.969938883312023*^9, 3.969938886805985*^9}, {
  3.969939236012437*^9, 
  3.969939239809753*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"1450ea87-9a5e-4dc7-ae42-\
3df885dafd79"],

Cell["\<\
We have reached the cusp of mathematics where computers are not just powerful \
tools but are integral. A lot of the proofs and the results are made by just \
running so many cases through a computer.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.969939181632592*^9, 
  3.969939211787053*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"b02292a8-3df5-46c4-b48c-\
d7174cb82af3"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Godel, turing, church via their incompleteness theorems, show that the very \
idea of trying to axiomatize mathematics is logically impossible in every \
order of logic. In 1956, newell, Simon, and Shaw devised the logical theory \
machine, which is automated theorem proving. Kevin Buzzard is doing the Xena \
project, to formalize all of mathematics. Using AI copilots for mathematics \
",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["-", TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "e49aa4f4-5316-4cd5-b003-67482ac4f035"],
 " we can look at Terrence tao over here."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9699392678355627`*^9, 3.9699393159899197`*^9}, {
  3.9699393530854073`*^9, 3.9699393901869287`*^9}, {3.9699394695035677`*^9, 
  3.969939485306897*^9}, {3.969939520217486*^9, 3.969939531767232*^9}, {
  3.969941275400218*^9, 
  3.969941275784018*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a464cfac-5f2a-4a58-8960-\
cd55c30e93f4"],

Cell["\<\
Can you do mathematics without actually understanding anything? This is very \
mathematical question and I think that any system which is not completely \
random has some patterns, found through exploration. And then these things \
are just exploited. So now we call them pattern matching, I call it the lack \
of randomness. And there is some sort of emotional response or something that \
guides our intuition.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.96993965058235*^9, 3.969939696786956*^9}, {
  3.96994013658016*^9, 3.969940155289659*^9}, {3.9699402564393787`*^9, 
  3.96994025974358*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"3472d2f7-0bdd-43e4-a027-\
b53f6bcec61a"],

Cell["\<\
There is this beautiful work of Chityoan et al in Nature, where they took all \
of material science, and they did a large language model for that, and \
generate new reactions n material science.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9699411050477343`*^9, 
  3.969941149415372*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"fb95ef60-dc6a-4e02-bd6a-\
55767c726d1e"],

Cell["\<\
Chat GPT passed the turing test in 2022. What is the turing test?\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.969941421602756*^9, 
  3.969941434051035*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"b3e1ca73-083f-43cb-b676-\
d507b65a1708"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Alpha proof and alpha geometry are training on so many problems specific to \
a certain thing for instance euclidean geometry. However, we might ask \
ourselves ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["-", TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "645ba317-2d5b-48f6-9e6e-94ecabe059e7"],
 " is pattern matching all that happens? If yes, aren\[CloseCurlyQuote]t \
computers just better than humans at solving these problems? Maybe."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.969941478963984*^9, 3.96994149329745*^9}, {
  3.969941760754764*^9, 3.969941810644863*^9}, {3.969941846248951*^9, 
  3.969941896634063*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"f35b431e-c4bb-4fa8-9118-\
d716d2618e8a"],

Cell["https://arxiv.org/pdf/1905.02263", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.969946849630014*^9},ExpressionUUID->"30ceb0b5-d773-4dc3-a11b-\
7b9b224402b1"],

Cell["https://arxiv.org/pdf/2312.05299", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.969946862095697*^9},ExpressionUUID->"4d30b4d6-51d2-42be-8196-\
12fff1e86e85"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "So professor Yang says that most mathematics and research happens in a \
top",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["-", TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "3b3c1214-e27a-4b84-915f-98d37d2ef911"],
 "down way; where we first have so many experiments and ideas and then once \
the story gets a little clear, then we actually start formalizing it. And \
this formalization is important because what happens is that as we read, we \
see that the system \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]just works\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] \
and there was certainly like a non linear process to make but it happened \
after several trials. Also, the bourbaki style presentation is very important \
for computers."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.969942089367289*^9, 3.9699421709364758`*^9}, {
   3.969942208547882*^9, 3.969942286139385*^9}, {3.9699423993096733`*^9, 
   3.969942399715294*^9}, 
   3.96994245907123*^9},ExpressionUUID->"1122847b-256d-4912-b6a8-\
88dfd2f1e69d"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The way the prime number theorem works was first discovered was by gauss \
just eyballling the number of primes less than some number and guessing. In \
fact, that\[CloseCurlyQuote]s how kepler came up with the equations of \
motion. And so these discoveries ",
 StyleBox["guide ",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "the actual formal discovery process because it reduces the randomeness and \
one would know what to think."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9699424018636436`*^9, 3.9699424203277903`*^9}, {
  3.969942461721528*^9, 
  3.969942536554022*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"eb3fecec-f201-49ce-9bc7-\
fb0cb66fd643"],

Cell["\<\
Here\[CloseCurlyQuote]s the thing he says: If it\[CloseCurlyQuote]s about \
just pattern matching, AI can do better than us. However, we need a lot of \
data to do that. So what? it\[CloseCurlyQuote]s worth it.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.969942792206307*^9, 3.969942809005887*^9}, {
  3.9699428408542433`*^9, 
  3.9699428587356977`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"8ed7d230-89d6-43ea-8226-\
cf1998476382"],

Cell["\<\
Peter Sarnak, a very good number theorist, was asking deep mind about \
liouville lambda of whether ddeepmind could do better than 50% for the \
sequence (because 50% is just random coin toss prediction).\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.969943108327381*^9, 3.96994316362662*^9}, 
   3.9699432110646257`*^9},ExpressionUUID->"86e68691-5306-40ca-b2f8-\
9c7dbb151dfc"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Teaching the AI", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9666552954926777`*^9, 
  3.966655301874654*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"81f4344b-4557-48e6-9fde-\
869a84575387"],

Cell["\<\
Seems that model theory is the \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]metamathematics of \
algebra\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote], which makes it seemingly very relevant to \
this course. \
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.969354665776086*^9, 3.969354668905051*^9}, {
  3.9693555134521837`*^9, 3.9693555389758663`*^9}, {3.9693556403639383`*^9, 
  3.9693556414135237`*^9}, {3.969356089360208*^9, 3.969356089491227*^9}, {
  3.970997360525242*^9, 3.970997363129423*^9}, {3.970997417723134*^9, 
  3.97099741787994*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"ce864861-c266-4220-95d5-\
5f87a302e68b"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Introduction", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9674603832245283`*^9, 3.967460385186872*^9}, {
  3.967463916444138*^9, 3.967463918710018*^9}, {3.967464046815649*^9, 
  3.96746404810473*^9}, {3.967529902812621*^9, 3.967529906960099*^9}, {
  3.9684874055535088`*^9, 
  3.968487407901084*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"7d96a30e-4cd0-45b9-95d8-\
258d9dabe3bd"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Q1",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ". We feel that the brain does not store everything and is somehow focused \
on \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]higher order structure\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]. Why \
does this help uncover patterns and contribute to understanding? If we \
focused on every detail, could we figure out overall patterns?"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.967530469616955*^9, 
  3.967530553381308*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"05c123b0-844c-4ef2-a251-\
e94b21c6a39e"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Q2",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ".",
 StyleBox[" ",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "Is a potential ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"brain", "-"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "9a9794e9-103b-4601-bfca-4cffc967a562"],
 "like higher level understanding the only way to display a sign of \
intelligence in a complex environment? If yes, then how can Chat-GPT work so \
well because it doesn\[CloseCurlyQuote]t really resemble a brain? If no, then \
what are the other ways? Is it specific to the type of system we are dealing \
with/trying to understand?"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.967530556624002*^9, 3.967530679303834*^9}, 
   3.967531070997473*^9, {3.967531598454845*^9, 3.967531614279202*^9}, {
   3.967545511882082*^9, 3.967545512666525*^9}, {3.968110598243101*^9, 
   3.968110600943959*^9}, {3.9682170093377447`*^9, 3.968217009664318*^9}, {
   3.968217598492085*^9, 
   3.968217598915699*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"b3d1e5fc-3a51-4a9c-9f84-\
b35b8405938a"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Q3.",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " Can there exist a mathematical framework for defining what patterns are, \
and how to exploit them? If no, then is there some broad categorisation?"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.968217039058191*^9, 3.96821703964844*^9}, {
   3.968218155965006*^9, 3.968218196510738*^9}, 
   3.968229503165607*^9},ExpressionUUID->"2fa733c5-4c6a-4d36-bc98-\
3db7bb6f7912"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Q1", "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.96753704111178*^9, 
  3.967537041337796*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"8a0458bd-7dbe-4aa8-ae72-\
b2abab4d711d"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The claim that higher order structure helps uncover patterns seems logical. \
However, we can\[CloseCurlyQuote]t seem to put this in words. Let\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]s come up with a definition of \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]higher",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["-", TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "955d4973-86a0-482d-9ed1-946357b7622f"],
 "order structure\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]. "
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.967530701229827*^9, 3.967530756445492*^9}, {
   3.967531148584868*^9, 3.9675312500193453`*^9}, {3.9675313598428783`*^9, 
   3.9675313601765947`*^9}, {3.967531581453236*^9, 3.9675315961337214`*^9}, {
   3.9675352340246162`*^9, 3.967535238217659*^9}, 3.967535752766983*^9, {
   3.9675359497491007`*^9, 3.9675359694932117`*^9}, {3.9675360045412207`*^9, 
   3.967536052452777*^9}, {3.967536291107356*^9, 3.967536322773563*^9}, {
   3.9675365105952797`*^9, 3.967536521810587*^9}, {3.9675366478375597`*^9, 
   3.967536651182498*^9}, {3.9675373330077*^9, 3.967537351605688*^9}, {
   3.967541581300171*^9, 
   3.9675415815324297`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"133ae6c9-d68b-4518-ae37-\
82d99c073b13"],

Cell["\<\
It seems that higher order structure refers to relationships or patterns \
between lower level elements. Higher order structure is the organization that \
becomes visible when one stops looking at isolated pieces and instead of the \
total picture. There is some data compression that enables similar features \
of different things/objects.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.967537356282236*^9, 3.967537374987603*^9}, {
  3.96753748274697*^9, 3.967537508595932*^9}, {3.967541586567891*^9, 
  3.967541628329123*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"f86e1112-32ac-4adb-ba37-\
57b781243a1f"],

Cell["There is a common theme here.", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.967541651941835*^9, 
  3.967541656053349*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"dd9d46d8-789a-4878-b956-\
0ae3066bd9da"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Example ",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "(Image recognition)",
 StyleBox[".",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " We see an image and we do not see every pixel, but the overall shape, the \
colour (roughly), brightness etc. and other things. Then only these \
compressed/higher order features are remembered. Then, if we see another \
picture, then these higher order features match which enable us to identify \
two images together. Theoretically speaking, the similarity in the 2 pictures \
in terms of pixel values is kind of weird not captured easily. However, we \
can figure out some features like how the borders are distinct in the images \
and then figure out the background things. And the borders are identified in \
terms of discontinuities in the pixel colour values. And the brain is using \
some sophisticated physical process to distinguish the process. "
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.967541641499538*^9, 3.967541662927517*^9}, {
  3.967541768409242*^9, 3.967541799371294*^9}, {3.967542141301546*^9, 
  3.967542156887927*^9}, {3.967542243433764*^9, 3.967542266513926*^9}, {
  3.9675425801967587`*^9, 3.96754263791457*^9}, {3.967542774095607*^9, 
  3.967542809761879*^9}, {3.967542861653899*^9, 3.967542912353277*^9}, {
  3.967542960974122*^9, 3.9675429686162643`*^9}, {3.9675431805940104`*^9, 
  3.967543196527375*^9}, {3.967543299844461*^9, 3.967543353459025*^9}, {
  3.970998024761076*^9, 
  3.970998053781301*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"736a781c-b8a7-48cf-9384-\
5436755a9f87"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Example ",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "(Mathematics). Mathematics is expressed in the language in set theory and \
technically every proof is a sequence of axioms and natural deduction. \
However, we don\[CloseCurlyQuote]t read proofs in that sense. There are some \
patterns which we always seem to identify, in different contexts. For \
instances, factoring integers. And in odd and even, we have that this is ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"2", "k"}], " ", "+", " ", "1"}], TraditionalForm]],
  ExpressionUUID->"068717b8-6eee-4247-9d23-70a721ae6fe6"],
 " etc. So obviously, linear independence is a notion that means that they \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]generate\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] something. In group \
theory the disjoint cycle proof was relying on some sort of intuition that we \
can multiply these things together. Basically, if we viewed everything as \
very axiomatic in terms of lean code/axioms, we will not be able to do many \
proofs as we will be lost in information."
}], "Text",
 InitializationCell->True,
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.967543543403559*^9, 3.967543549806151*^9}, {
  3.967543585007879*^9, 3.967543616346085*^9}, {3.967543663640972*^9, 
  3.967543670123949*^9}, {3.967543809744097*^9, 3.9675438321332273`*^9}, {
  3.967543902971971*^9, 3.967543908296875*^9}, {3.967543960422723*^9, 
  3.967543963189204*^9}, {3.967545555383399*^9, 3.967545653580669*^9}, {
  3.967545748342366*^9, 3.967545761490901*^9}, {3.967545821173777*^9, 
  3.967545841356809*^9}, {3.967545932986367*^9, 3.967546120624619*^9}, {
  3.967546495292756*^9, 
  3.967546496144417*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"093ea269-1013-4f0f-b951-\
7e5489024339"],

Cell["\<\
Technically, if we did figure out every detail, then we should be able to get \
the higher level understanding because the higher level understanding is a \
function of the every detail. So we are done by the data processing \
inequality. Although there might not be any general framework to formalize \
the existence of patterns, we can think of it as data compression. Now, what \
to compress etc. is the major part of the pattern recognition process. Also, \
maybe the higher order structure itself is the existence of patterns! In \
pictures of objects, the common thing is the border and everthing ETC.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.968111327661351*^9, 3.9681113946575527`*^9}, {
  3.968112270876128*^9, 3.96811227668508*^9}, {3.968112433576148*^9, 
  3.968112493278261*^9}, {3.968173244671158*^9, 
  3.96817327913491*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"57f04f8b-c638-4d98-87ee-\
6d1a7b11e308"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Q2", "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.96811256889844*^9, 
  3.9681125690701437`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"4d6cd14e-642c-4a1b-aa8e-\
8e4df4c047b8"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The claim that focusing on higher order structure i the only way to display \
intelligence",
 StyleBox[" seems",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " true if we consider higher order structure as just figuring out patterns. \
However, does ChatGPT explicitly try to detect patterns? Well, on some level, \
yes. Because it uses an embedding to exploit the fact that there are patterns \
and then the neural network is guaranteed to find them in a sense. Thus, the \
important idea is to find the embedding. "
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.968173290064404*^9, 3.968173348380787*^9}, {
  3.968173395900027*^9, 3.968173405598301*^9}, {3.968173660190276*^9, 
  3.968173661316542*^9}, {3.968173910757702*^9, 3.968173954143176*^9}, {
  3.9709969010364037`*^9, 
  3.9709969017911787`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"673e316b-373d-4dba-8cd2-\
d9b0139abbe0"],

Cell["\<\
So in GPT, we don\[CloseCurlyQuote]t know what the neural net is \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]thinking\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote], but we do know that \
there are enough parameters that it is implicitly figuring out those \
patterns. Thus, a higher order structure is just the existence of patterns \
and some sort of regularity. And inevitably that means that if patterns \
exist, so do relations between items on a smaller level. \
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.968173963637466*^9, 3.968173982477738*^9}, {
  3.968196847680039*^9, 3.968196853447446*^9}, {3.968197264942422*^9, 
  3.9681973181411676`*^9}, {3.968197467393176*^9, 
  3.968197492987537*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"f14867c8-4e78-430a-b1b2-\
8f535a7ac19a"],

Cell["\<\
A lower level model is somewhat inevitable because we are receiving every \
input. As a person, it feels that i don\[CloseCurlyQuote]t have access to \
that processing and I just know that it is \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]a bird\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] or something roughly speaking. Thus, we should make \
a model whose consequence is that feeling.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.968197523840836*^9, 3.968197586938461*^9}, {
  3.970996921625811*^9, 
  3.9709969259876003`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a320d504-f947-4258-a9e7-\
48f80031e426"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Example",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " (English language). "
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.967546094219266*^9, 3.9675460991351547`*^9}, {
  3.967546690352188*^9, 
  3.967546692910502*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"78c139c4-dbca-4952-bc36-\
6b9f330807dc"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We can give examples. For example, in proving that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{" ", 
    RowBox[{
     SuperscriptBox["n", "3"], " ", "-", " ", "n"}]}], TraditionalForm]],
  ExpressionUUID->"29cb3ab9-1d0b-4cab-af99-bf3eca3a63c4"],
 " is a multiple of 3, it is immediately obvious to factor this because \
somehow we quickly know that it \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]might get us somewhere\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote].  In analysis, what often \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]helps\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] is to visualise a \
sequence converging, which helps us understand why this makes \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]sense\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]. And then we know that \
to show convergence of sum of sequences we can use the triangle inequality \
because we can think ahead and somehow...that concept is applicable. In a \
general level, we can think of it as some sort of clustering or similarity. "
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.96753735081443*^9, 
  3.967537355034717*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"e24d0e6a-6f2c-4bff-8d68-\
018925c99886"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Definition ",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "(Intelligence).",
 StyleBox[" ",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "Intelligence is the ability to find regularities in a complex system by \
interacting with the system and/or getting feedback from it."
}], "Item",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9666715286271152`*^9, 3.96667153054558*^9}, {
  3.966671758611851*^9, 3.966671802498605*^9}, {3.966671845037709*^9, 
  3.966671942798354*^9}, {3.966672020242608*^9, 3.966672054870279*^9}, {
  3.96667233557206*^9, 3.966672337374854*^9}, {3.966672373666333*^9, 
  3.966672376612417*^9}, {3.966741292770586*^9, 3.966741293803418*^9}, {
  3.9667413245423813`*^9, 3.966741325480653*^9}, {3.966771412296574*^9, 
  3.966771460989421*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"3c5ade9a-6b80-4843-9db5-\
653f3b80813a"],

Cell["\<\
This means there exists regularities in the structure right? what does that \
mean? Regularities can mean a lot of things depending on the mathematical \
framework. It can be continuity. It can mean a probability distribution or \
the ability to ensure correctness with time on average. Something like that. \
\
\>", "Subitem",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9666715286271152`*^9, 3.96667153054558*^9}, {
  3.966671758611851*^9, 3.966671802498605*^9}, {3.966671845037709*^9, 
  3.966671942798354*^9}, {3.966672020242608*^9, 3.966672054870279*^9}, {
  3.96667233557206*^9, 3.966672337374854*^9}, {3.966672373666333*^9, 
  3.966672376612417*^9}, {3.966741292770586*^9, 3.966741293803418*^9}, {
  3.9667413245423813`*^9, 3.966741325480653*^9}, {3.966771412296574*^9, 
  3.966771460835639*^9}, {3.9667718026881647`*^9, 3.966771860054384*^9}, {
  3.966827662914912*^9, 3.966827709288272*^9}, {3.966827742440052*^9, 
  3.966827762501432*^9}, {3.966827855014963*^9, 
  3.966827857114826*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"bb14d6e1-4bfe-4549-89e2-\
b043e9b33888"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.967530428802651*^9, 
  3.9675304428396897`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"7cd431bd-121f-4935-bb07-\
c27d74c75c4e"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Q3", "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.968229507545258*^9, 3.968229507736892*^9}, {
  3.9683011851802893`*^9, 
  3.968301185465272*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"fdc951be-31e7-4715-a9b4-\
e739ed438e28"],

Cell["\<\
This is a very hard question and it is quite hard to answer. Because a \
general mathematical framework seems very difficult to discover. Thinking \
about it in categorical terms etc. is useful but not in the immediate short \
term.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9683967580788507`*^9, 3.9683967789822683`*^9}, {
  3.968397606598775*^9, 
  3.968397669351233*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"231a2a08-1203-4dd5-bf5e-\
ce70d8631c53"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Patterns seem to be a lack of randomness. If we have a random variable, it \
can take many different values. For instance, consider the joint random \
variable ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"X", " ", "=", " ", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{
      SubscriptBox["X", "1"], "..."}], ".", 
     SubscriptBox["X", "N"]}]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "4775be25-a8c2-4365-a5f2-3c4ed415e489"],
 " where each ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["X", "i"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "6a040cae-e89b-4b24-85eb-a4db6ddfc059"],
 " is a random coin toss and are i.i.d. Then ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["X", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "2b050852-1260-4699-8eb2-2bf3a2cd5530"],
 " is genuinely something random i.e we cannot predict the next coin toss \
because they are all independent of each other. However, if we say that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"Z", " ", "=", " ", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{
       SubscriptBox["X", "1"], "+"}], " ", "..."}], ".", " ", 
     RowBox[{"+", " ", 
      SubscriptBox["X", "N"]}]}]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "71d69aa1-1a3b-41bb-aba8-035f3106eabf"],
 ", then ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["Z", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "c8a837fc-a01f-4480-80a8-ea1f689a409b"],
 " follows the binomial distribution and so some values are more probable \
than the others. Well, this is just a consequence of the fact that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"H", "(", 
     RowBox[{"f", "(", "X", ")"}], ")"}], "<=", " ", 
    RowBox[{"H", "(", "X", ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "490ebcaf-9aae-46b3-9411-edb3f9fd046f"],
 " for any function ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["f", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "f0ccbc4f-6c0d-4c0a-9e01-7af692c333b6"],
 "! And so the randomness reduces."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.968397673353947*^9, 3.968397704446582*^9}, {
   3.968397742775248*^9, 3.968397754694805*^9}, {3.968405816075669*^9, 
   3.968405824007626*^9}, {3.9684059869888563`*^9, 3.968405998309566*^9}, {
   3.968406139540633*^9, 3.968406156195957*^9}, {3.968406197294911*^9, 
   3.968406207815083*^9}, {3.968406362163362*^9, 3.968406367744631*^9}, {
   3.968406442061816*^9, 3.968406443064896*^9}, {3.968406545648035*^9, 
   3.96840655125881*^9}, {3.968407065320841*^9, 3.96840707446998*^9}, {
   3.968411833964225*^9, 3.968411848790512*^9}, {3.968412919925141*^9, 
   3.9684129200657063`*^9}, {3.9684139400947104`*^9, 3.968413944416706*^9}, {
   3.968414220327613*^9, 3.9684142507672997`*^9}, {3.96841431828907*^9, 
   3.968414319722956*^9}, {3.968414358375985*^9, 3.968414482630197*^9}, 
   3.968414566094091*^9, {3.968414642294161*^9, 3.968414741476508*^9}, {
   3.968414941063748*^9, 
   3.968414944870919*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"d06df454-4a82-4121-97bd-\
580a5951273f"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "At the end of the day, this is about learning a ",
 StyleBox["function ",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "i.e. an ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"input", "-", "output"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "9008f428-5032-471a-9e46-cc9e52178ef2"],
 " mapping using some examples. And the existence of a ",
 StyleBox["pattern ",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "means that the entire function can be specified or approximated by looking \
at a subset of examples. Thus, if I have a real polynomial and i know the \
degree ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["d", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "12ff26c9-8e23-4cf5-9fc6-c6612a8aab6d"],
 ", i need ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["log", "2"], "d"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "ea67e323-d47b-4360-ba09-10b02041c7a2"],
 " bits of information or something to know the function entirely. And in the \
case of a neural network, we deal with functions like that. ",
 StyleBox["However, there must be some theory of embeddings where the space \
in which the function is represented is invariant to how much we can learn \
from that function. ",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "This has to do with representation theory where the learnability is \
invaraint of the representation (related to the theory of characters and \
other things). The idea is that we can choose a topology so that the \
functions are continuous. ==...but they have to be compatible..hmm...ideas of \
sheaves...and atlas and other things are coming to me now. "
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.968485728165943*^9, 3.968485761717005*^9}, {
  3.96848584266894*^9, 3.968485856907518*^9}, {3.968485902569105*^9, 
  3.968486005112494*^9}, {3.968486078282913*^9, 3.968486138249849*^9}, {
  3.968486740776175*^9, 3.968486794566924*^9}, {3.96848693321484*^9, 
  3.9684869522525187`*^9}, {3.968487009783927*^9, 3.968487058034719*^9}, {
  3.968487172985693*^9, 3.968487177525733*^9}, {3.968487390587239*^9, 
  3.968487391189804*^9}, {3.968565050527292*^9, 
  3.968565050945272*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"dea81097-48a1-4961-bdca-\
ad3c0387623b"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "For instance, let\[CloseCurlyQuote]s look at the double slit experiment. It \
seems random at first. However, if we do the trials again and again we can \
see that it is less random. Thus, the output is a random variable which means \
it is not determinstic but ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"H", "(", "X", ")"}], "<=", 
    RowBox[{"H", "(", "Unif", ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "94b94c27-023b-4e5f-83a3-38d033f19316"],
 " where ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["Unif", TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "f96986f4-55ea-4654-a005-5f865d3d05be"],
 " is the uniform distribution on the sample space. "
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.968583886042103*^9, 3.968583963314299*^9}, {
  3.9685840475859833`*^9, 
  3.9685840478643417`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"48a43735-e445-40ab-93aa-\
bac4a0ec0b34"],

Cell["\<\
An Introduction to Kolmogorov Complexity and Its Applications. \
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9684867371790133`*^9, 
  3.9684867383142014`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"15662f94-c87c-41aa-92e7-\
048d6f7ad374"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Ok so here is a task we can think about. We have to learn a random variable \
",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"X", " ", ":", "\[CapitalOmega]"}], " ", "->", " ", 
    "\[DoubleStruckCapitalR]"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "8cb1d48f-50c8-4bff-abdd-dfa257e5c68c"],
 ". For instance if ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"\[CapitalOmega]", " ", "=", " ", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"0", ",", "1"}], "}"}]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "d488b12f-3265-438f-b8d6-c127c0137f8b"],
 " where ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["0", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "aa7fb788-1bbc-49c6-a90b-dd5d9c903f13"],
 " = heads and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"1", " ", "=", " "}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "a95bb0c3-118f-4463-8912-f1b96744ae76"],
 "tails and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["X", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "663af9ba-8f48-455c-b22d-db40b4dc4453"],
 " is the indicator random variable of getting heads, then there is a pattern \
that we can notice pretty easily. "
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.968414946275071*^9, 3.968414966802064*^9}, {
  3.968426933987329*^9, 3.968426938990504*^9}, {3.9684851022764606`*^9, 
  3.968485148777813*^9}, {3.96848532575637*^9, 3.968485357952784*^9}, {
  3.968485401328994*^9, 3.968485448816131*^9}, {3.968485569346024*^9, 
  3.968485580891163*^9}, {3.968485610904388*^9, 3.968485622681727*^9}, {
  3.968485726012573*^9, 3.968485726183893*^9}, {3.968565087207751*^9, 
  3.968565087345921*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"e7047eda-c729-4bad-8253-\
630f5852e3fb"],

Cell["\<\
This precision think is bothering me. For instance, if there is an image of a \
bat, I am thinking that there is a handle and the overall shape. Now, I am \
trying to understand how can this unprecise understanding be implemented. \
After all, everything starts from a lower level. And we \
don\[CloseCurlyQuote]t remember the full image...just the important \
features...like the handle and other things...e etc...something which is a \
distinguishing feature. Now that there is a bat. How ot implement this as a \
computer. forget the brain. hmm. Interesting.People must have thought about \
this. Well, there is a lot to get through.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.968381780487924*^9, 3.968381799651511*^9}, {
  3.968382012185383*^9, 3.968382021926168*^9}, {3.968382133099148*^9, 
  3.968382166582476*^9}, {3.968382375760192*^9, 3.9683824013610992`*^9}, {
  3.968382560053731*^9, 3.968382572623611*^9}, {3.968382645885993*^9, 
  3.968382678708109*^9}, {3.968383407776886*^9, 3.96838342860017*^9}, {
  3.96838347216514*^9, 3.968383483246185*^9}, {3.9683840984954*^9, 
  3.968384103236882*^9}, {3.969175759512657*^9, 
  3.969175760155579*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"be841bfd-a057-490e-a18a-\
a1d011bbe60b"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Sparse coding is a technique that is used where we are representing an \
image using a combination of basis functions, each of which are representing \
some edges, blobs etc. and the important features. Also, it turns out that \
the visual cortex does actually process a large amount of data and has \
different layers which filter out the data. It will be useful to read how \
that works. However, the good thing about neural nets is that there exists \
some pre",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["-", TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "f3b3ae93-e8f8-403c-b7b0-2d3b2117bb37"],
 "defined embedding input output. So it;s a wonderful type thing over here."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.968396265190876*^9, 3.968396279292777*^9}, {
  3.968396541512917*^9, 3.968396618693631*^9}, {3.969175763719067*^9, 
  3.969175771450523*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"1491bdd9-87ac-436e-8edf-\
ad766571055b"],

Cell["\<\
I think that the AI can leverage physical phenomena like quantum computing \
etc. Why not? This is because well, yeah. \
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.968377091668667*^9, 
  3.968377108623678*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"9d622776-fbd7-45bb-988b-\
33a4141f168d"],

Cell["\<\
It seems that the patterns just come to us in our brain. But we don\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]t know what processing actually takes place. And a \
mathematical formalism to define what they actually are seems very complex \
because there are such a wide variety of systems. Thus, maybe we have to \
define what a system is, which requires a lot of effort as well because it \
can be anything.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.968229513401691*^9, 3.968229526841895*^9}, 
   3.968229680451919*^9, {3.9682298426487293`*^9, 3.968229890558178*^9}, {
   3.968229925406679*^9, 3.96822997672331*^9}, {3.9682302071859922`*^9, 
   3.968230254868811*^9}, {3.968230578725865*^9, 
   3.968230585174034*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"7c45f433-651c-4f0c-a9f1-\
c1bc692fc057"],

Cell["\<\
A generic paradigm to exploit patterns can be neural networks. Here, the \
existence of patterns etc. implies that there exists an embedding of the \
input data and the resulting function can be approximated by a somewhat \
continuous function. That is why Chat GPT etc. works. For instance, in image \
recognition, a feature that is relevant is the border or the immediate change \
in pixel colour values. Then, in a border detection algorithm, we can use a \
neural net that somehow encodes that in the neural network formulation over \
here. SO yeah. \
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9682307755001993`*^9, 3.968230783666616*^9}, {
   3.968230826576331*^9, 3.968230826755178*^9}, {3.968231111593688*^9, 
   3.9682311184370823`*^9}, 3.968291283559157*^9, {3.968291403093398*^9, 
   3.9682914232938843`*^9}, {3.9682916198998013`*^9, 
   3.9682916571144323`*^9}, {3.9682918248291817`*^9, 3.968291831439989*^9}, {
   3.96829205398958*^9, 3.968292058437632*^9}, {3.968292091050107*^9, 
   3.9682921978637323`*^9}, {3.968292331409923*^9, 3.968292337787004*^9}, {
   3.968292606464087*^9, 
   3.9682926165597*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"ea40768c-7199-4911-a471-\
4ed7e4f5dc47"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

