Cell[TextData[{
 "In ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["\[DoubleStruckCapitalR]", "n"], TraditionalForm]],
  ExpressionUUID->"d98c36e0-f676-4566-8a9d-3d957c57d348"],
 ", how much information can we capture? it seems like the metric i.e. \
distance between things is a differentiating feature. we can think of groups \
or something else maye or things ust related to each other in some way. \
However, there is a limit to much how can we embed the information here. \
because if ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "8b868de0-4703-480d-a4a3-8723075128b2"],
 " concepts are unrelated, then they are perpneidcular...however if we get \
another unrealted cnocept..it cannot be perpendicular...this is somwhat \
constraining.  We have a continuous concept of entropy as well! Wow...okay. \
This is interesting. I love information theory. hmm. Well,nothing like this \
is easy at all. I believe in the compounding effect basically. There must be \
some sort of an intuition for the data being stored acorss the real numbers \
and how much information we can tehcnically convey...there must be some \
mathematics. "
}], "Item",
 InitializationCell->True,
