Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Parser", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.965958834196751*^9, 
  3.965958842183269*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"4debef1c-1b7c-4405-8616-\
82da3b531860"],

Cell["\<\
Hmm. can we just use an LLM API or something. Let\[CloseCurlyQuote]s try. Let\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]s come up with an intermediate representation. It can be a \
proof checker or something. There are many ideas. One idea is that we first \
make the computer understand how to read math in general to get an idea. Now, \
we can make it very output based so that it just gives the output, which is a \
NLP task and will require us to train it on a large amount of data with input \
output pairs; which will be very painful because we\[CloseCurlyQuote]ll have \
to make the intermediate representation. There must be another ML algorithm \
for this. Okay. \
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.965961369443802*^9, 3.965961370987182*^9}, {
   3.965972498624443*^9, 3.965972546585904*^9}, {3.965972706355876*^9, 
   3.965972712132076*^9}, {3.965975177916941*^9, 3.9659752278597517`*^9}, {
   3.965975324293254*^9, 3.965975365765625*^9}, {3.965975439072544*^9, 
   3.965975446459195*^9}, {3.965975715536113*^9, 3.965975762620839*^9}, 
   3.9659758200443707`*^9, {3.965975888046514*^9, 3.965975892768672*^9}, {
   3.966054532735402*^9, 
   3.966054534080851*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a2b3dd94-e4cc-4d26-aa2c-\
ff2a1c4bc9bb"],

Cell["\<\
What can I do. I can make some agent to teach it mathematics on my own and I \
can be a teacher. Maybe. Who knows. I don\[CloseCurlyQuote]t know.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.965975786452048*^9, 
  3.965975817137692*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"076941c6-b0e6-45a1-a582-\
6492c78def06"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Teaching Logical structures of proofs", "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.966054721856771*^9, 
  3.966054729888896*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"5f83ee0f-2de6-479f-8beb-\
e417a0836980"],

Cell["\<\
Well, since proofs can pretty complicated, if it understands the logical \
structure of a proof, then it should understand the proof also. Because a \
proof is just a bunch of arguments together etc. and each step is somewhat of \
a logical step. The training data will be English proofs and their \
corresponding IR representations, and we simply cannot curate that amount of \
data. Also, if we are able to make an AI that can read a proof in natural \
language (which is written sufficiently well) and convert it into lean code, \
it should be able to understand the proof because there are many implicit \
steps in a proof that require some mathematical knowledge. \
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.966054864187521*^9, 3.966054926028829*^9}, {
  3.966054964437273*^9, 3.9660549883233223`*^9}, {3.966064666902932*^9, 
  3.9660646768354077`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"8c982c4a-87b6-47f9-8858-\
756aecd6ac24"],

Cell["\<\
For instance, I was reading the group theory proofs and realised that the \
proofs are not that complex precisely because I have the intuition and can \
quickly notice the important. Converting it into lean code is easy but very \
tedious because there are so many small things that a human becomes \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]confident\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] in over time.  SO \
does an AI need that confidence? Probably not because we have ChatGPT that \
can do the same thing.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.966063936549985*^9, 3.966063947237052*^9}, {
   3.966064086583547*^9, 3.966064092179629*^9}, {3.966064129067664*^9, 
   3.966064223660115*^9}, 3.966064660675809*^9, 3.966065383229748*^9, {
   3.966217791665098*^9, 3.966217805655389*^9}, {3.966217850616407*^9, 
   3.96621786057004*^9}, {3.966217921882044*^9, 3.966217955344747*^9}, {
   3.966218252346946*^9, 3.966218274675033*^9}, {3.96621969176962*^9, 
   3.966219715875231*^9}, {3.966219752982671*^9, 
   3.9662197531266317`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"479afd47-e40b-417c-a5e8-\
7ddfd8c3d22a"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Bootstrapping data", "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9660651166965513`*^9, 
  3.966065122137639*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"e149f3e5-7e8f-451a-af17-\
938a3564bba3"],

Cell["\<\
Generate Pseudo-English summaries of lean proofs as training data. Then train \
an English to Lean model. This would be interesting to do and a nice project. \
However, it doesn\[CloseCurlyQuote]t really focus on the AI learning aspect I\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]m talking about. However, it can be a good step to think \
about over here.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.966065123978389*^9, 3.966065196552474*^9}, {
  3.966065387465653*^9, 
  3.966065401647532*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"49e0b608-f534-4682-99e3-\
b403fe35dbc9"],

Cell["\<\
Let\[CloseCurlyQuote]s try this. I guess learning about random algorithms is \
going to be fun and also can use the chat gpt open AI or something or modify \
it a bit so that it can do these proofs. Hmm. Interesting. We can procure a \
range of training data from randomizing the english sentences, all of which \
can be done. We can try. Hmm. However, there\[CloseCurlyQuote]s more \
structure to learn here instead of the next token. The next token will \
probably not be sufficient.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.966219975982996*^9, 3.966219986796219*^9}, {
  3.966222532674801*^9, 3.966222543266918*^9}, {3.966516758683251*^9, 
  3.966516843470252*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"dde5fd52-7e86-4dcf-a3ea-\
8c9d9e395971"]
}, Open  ]],

